Negative control for the BDD anchor gate.

The gate's job is to catch a capability that is DECLARED and never proved — a
`.feature` scenario nobody anchors a spec to. That gap is invisible by
construction: the feature file reads complete, the suite is green, and the only
thing missing is the link between them.

The mutant declares one more capability on the session-switcher feature and
anchors no spec to it. Nothing else changes: the existing scenarios keep their
specs, so a suite that graded only "are the specs green" grades this a pass.

The mutant adds rather than deletes on purpose. Deleting an `@anchor` from a
spec would mutate a TEST, which is the prover's lane and proves nothing about
production; a feature file is the capability declaration itself, so adding to it
is a source mutation the gate must answer for.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Expected RED (module lane):
  feature-traceability.spec.ts  "leaves no declared capability without a spec
                                  naming it"
                                -> ["…/session-switcher.feature @T-4"]

The sibling assertion ("leaves no spec anchor pointing at a capability its
feature never declared") stays GREEN — it walks the other direction, and no
spec gained an anchor. Both directions have to exist, and only one of them can
see this mutant.

